---
// A replication note. /contribute/ says a replication "publishes as its own note",
// and this is what that looks like: the finding is the product, and the explorer at
// /notes/cascade-explorer/ is the same model made interrogable rather than a separate
// instrument.
//
// Every number is read from the records the pipeline wrote — gates.json for the
// pre-registered checks, findings.json for what surfaced afterwards — so the prose
// cannot drift from the run.
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import G from "../../data/cascade-cold.json";
import F from "../../data/cascade-findings.json";

const gates = G.gates;
const g = (id) => gates.find((x) => x.id === id);
const f3 = (n) => (n == null ? "—" : Number(n).toFixed(3));
const usd = (n) => "$" + Number(n).toLocaleString("en-US");
const failed = gates.filter((x) => x.status === "fail");
const passed = gates.filter((x) => x.status === "pass");
const implied = g("G-3").implied;
const costs = g("G-4").per_mixture;
const common = g("G-5").per_mixture;
const winner = Object.keys(common).reduce((a, b) => (common[a].best_cop > common[b].best_cop ? a : b));

// findings that surfaced only when the envelope was made recomputable
const vac = F.sub_atmospheric_low_side.per_mixture;
const vacMix = Object.keys(vac).filter((m) => vac[m].sub_atmospheric > 0);
const dtI = F.cost_minimising_cascade_approach.inferred_coefficient;
const dtP = F.cost_minimising_cascade_approach.printed_coefficient;
const pr = F.pressure_ratio_range;
const spur = Object.entries(F.property_solver.per_mixture)
  .filter(([, r]) => r.beat_carnot > 0);
const pct = (x) => Math.round(x * 100) + "%";
---

<Page
  title="Replication note — a cascade refrigeration optimisation"
  description="Rebuilding a published thermoeconomic optimisation from the paper alone: what reproduced, what did not, and a compressor efficiency recovered from the paper's own reported COP."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Replication note · no data or code from the authors</p>
    <h1>Three of five checks reproduced.<br /><em>Two did not.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A published optimisation of a −80 °C cascade refrigeration system, rebuilt from the
      paper's own equations and re-scored against the numbers it reports. It needed nothing
      from the authors — no dataset, no code, no permission. Two of the five pre-registered
      checks failed, and one of the failures is about the paper's method rather than ours.
    </p>
    <dl class="tally">
      <div><dt>Checks passed</dt><dd>{passed.length}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Checks failed</dt><dd class="want">{failed.length}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>From the authors</dt><dd>0</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Recovered</dt><dd>η<sub>is</sub></dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Source<span>what was replicated</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <p>
        {G.replicates.citation}, doi:<a href={`https://doi.org/${G.replicates.doi}`}>{G.replicates.doi}</a>,
        published open access under {G.replicates.licence}.
      </p>
      <p>
        Propane in the high-temperature circuit, a CO₂/hydrocarbon mixture in the low
        circuit, reaching −80 °C for biomedical cold storage. Two objectives: maximise
        exergetic efficiency, minimise total annual cost, optimised with a genetic algorithm
        over four operating temperatures.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>Nothing here is copied.</b> The cycle was rebuilt from the equations and
          assumptions the paper states, with <a href="http://www.coolprop.org/">CoolProp</a>
          in place of REFPROP. No figure is reproduced and no table is republished as data —
          the licence forbids derivatives, and the paper's reported values appear only as
          thresholds this implementation is scored against, which is ordinary scholarly
          comparison. Generated {G.generated.slice(0, 10)} with CoolProp {G.coolprop_version}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Recovered<span>a number its author no longer had</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <h2>The compressor efficiency, back-solved from the paper's own COP</h2>
      <p>
        The paper says compression is "expressed as a function of pressure ratio" and never
        gives the function. The author confirmed he no longer has it. Rather than assume
        one, the cycle was inverted: what isentropic efficiency reproduces the COP the paper
        reports, at the operating point it reports?
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Low-circuit mixture</th><th>Implied η<sub>is</sub></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {Object.entries(implied).map(([m, v]) => (
              <tr><td>CO₂/{m}</td><td class="num">{f3(v)}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Three different working fluids agree to <b>±0.01</b>. That agreement is the evidence:
        a cycle wrong in a fluid-dependent way would scatter. <b>The compressor was running
        near η<sub>is</sub> ≈ 0.65</b>, and that is now a recoverable fact rather than a lost one.
      </p>
      <p class="dim">
        The exergetic efficiency definition was missing too, and was resolved rather than
        guessed: COP divided by the Carnot COP reproduces the paper's own 22–35% Pareto axis
        from its own reported COP range of 0.40–0.65.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Checks<span>thresholds fixed before the runs</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Hard</th><th>Outcome</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {gates.map((x) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="num">{x.id}</td>
                <td class="dim">{x.hard ? "hard" : "soft"}</td>
                <td class={x.status === "pass" ? "yes" : "no"}>{x.status}</td>
                <td>{x.name}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Failure one<span>the composition rule</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <h2>The optimum mixtures cannot be reproduced, because the rule is not published</h2>
      <p>
        The paper reports optimum compositions of 94%, 64% and 37% CO₂ by mass. Our
        COP maximum sits at an endpoint of the composition range for all three mixtures.
        The reason is in the paper's own methods: composition was chosen by
        "maximizing COP, provided that the carbon dioxide did not undergo crystallization
        and values capable of burning of hydrocarbons were reduced".
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Neither the crystallisation limit nor the flammability criterion is quantified
        anywhere in the paper.</b> So the reported optima are constrained choices whose
        constraints are not published. An ideal-solubility freeze-out limit does not
        reproduce them either.
      </p>
      <p class="dim">
        The dew-point convention used here does reproduce the qualitative shape of all three
        published curves, including the mid-composition dip — which also explains why one
        printed figure shows a negative COP. That region is where the evaporator enthalpy
        rise collapses and the cycle stops closing; those points are recorded as infeasible
        here rather than plotted as a negative number.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Failure two<span>the mixture ranking</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <h2>The three mixtures were compared at three different temperatures</h2>
      <p>
        The paper's composition scans are captioned at <b>−80 °C</b>, <b>−85 °C</b> and
        <b>−82 °C</b> respectively, and it then compares those curves to select CO₂/ethylene.
        Re-scanned at a common −80 °C:
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Mixture</th><th>Best COP at −80 °C</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {Object.entries(common).map(([m, v]) => (
              <tr>
                <td>CO₂/{m}{m === winner ? " — best" : ""}</td>
                <td class="num">{f3(v.best_cop)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        At a shared evaporation temperature the best mixture is <b>CO₂/{winner}</b>, not
        ethylene. This was registered in advance as the check most likely to fail while
        everything else passed, and it did.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Cost<span>the printed equation</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <h2>As printed, the cost correlations give 2.4× the paper's own reported range</h2>
      <p>
        Implementing every correlation exactly as given, the paper's three reported optima
        cost far more than the Pareto range the paper itself publishes. It localises to one
        term — the cascade exchanger, whose small approach temperature implies a large area.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Mixture</th><th>As printed</th><th>Coefficient ÷ 10</th><th>Paper's own range</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {Object.entries(costs).map(([m, c]) => (
              <tr>
                <td>CO₂/{m}</td>
                <td class="no num">{usd(c.as_printed_usd)}</td>
                <td class="yes num">{usd(c.reconstructed_usd)}</td>
                <td class="num dim">{usd(c.band[0])}–{usd(c.band[1])}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Reducing that one coefficient tenfold puts all three inside the published bands,
        which is consistent with a lost decimal point. <b>That is our inference, not the
        paper's statement</b> — adopted with the author's agreement, and both numbers are
        published so a reader can take either.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Afterwards<span>what the model already knew</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <h2>Four things nobody had asked the model</h2>
      <p>
        The five checks above were fixed before the runs. These came later, when the
        explorer was rewritten to recompute the envelope instead of interpolating a
        sampled one. None of them is a verdict on the paper; all four were sitting
        inside the model from the first run, unasked.
      </p>

      <h3>The low side runs under vacuum — but only for the mixture the paper did not pick</h3>
      <p>
        The paper reports no circuit pressures, so this is invisible in it. At a
        {F.sub_atmospheric_low_side.at_condenser_c} °C condenser and a
        {F.sub_atmospheric_low_side.at_dt_k} K approach, CO₂/{vacMix.join(" and CO₂/")}
        sits below atmospheric on the low-circuit suction at
        {pct(vac[vacMix[0]].share)} of its feasible operating points, down to
        {vac[vacMix[0]].suction_bar_range[0]} bar absolute. A circuit under vacuum at
        standstill draws air and moisture in through any leak, which is the ordinary way
        a −80 °C plant dies. The other two mixtures never do it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That matters because of check G-5. At a common evaporation temperature the best
        COP belongs to CO₂/{winner} — and CO₂/{winner} is also the one that goes
        sub-atmospheric. A comparison on COP alone would recommend the fluid with the
        design problem.
      </p>

      <h3>The disputed cost coefficient does the opposite of what it looks like</h3>
      <p>
        A reader could reasonably suspect that dividing the cascade-exchanger cost
        coefficient by ten lets the optimiser buy a huge exchanger and sit at the
        smallest approach. It does not. Over all
        {dtI.combinations.toLocaleString()} combinations of mixture and the other three
        decisions, the cost-minimising approach is
        {dtI.range_k[0]}–{dtI.range_k[1]} K with the coefficient we inferred — most often
        {dtI.most_common_k} K, in {pct(dtI.most_common_share)} of cases — which is
        squarely inside ordinary industrial practice. With the coefficient
        <em>as printed</em> it is {dtP.range_k[0]} K in
        {pct(dtP.most_common_share)} of cases: pinned to the top of the range.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the paper's own reported optima at 1.5–1.8 K are not cost minima under either
        coefficient. They are points chosen at the efficiency end of a two-objective
        front, which is a legitimate choice — and now a visible one.
      </p>

      <h3>One compressor efficiency covers a ninefold spread of pressure ratio</h3>
      <p>
        Across {pr.points.toLocaleString()} operating points the low circuit's pressure
        ratio runs {pr.low_circuit[0].toFixed(1)} to {pr.low_circuit[1].toFixed(1)} and
        the high circuit's {pr.high_circuit[0].toFixed(1)} to
        {pr.high_circuit[1].toFixed(1)}. A single flat η<sub>is</sub> of
        0.65 is applied to all of them. Real isentropic efficiency falls as pressure
        ratio rises, so the coldest part of the envelope is flattered by an amount this
        implementation cannot quantify — the paper said it had that function and never
        printed it. This is the weakest assumption in the model, and it is ours as much
        as the paper's.
      </p>

      <h3>The property library returns a spurious root, and it was setting the front</h3>
      <p>
        {F.property_solver.library} fails to converge at isolated nodes for the two
        mixtures whose partner sits nearest its own critical temperature. Worse, at
        {spur.map(([m, r]) => `${r.beat_carnot} nodes for CO₂/${m}`).join(" and ")} it
        returns a saturated-liquid enthalpy roughly 700 kJ/kg out of line with its
        neighbour one kelvin away, which produced a COP of 1.73 at −80 °C — 94% of the
        Carnot ceiling for a cascade with a 0.65 compressor. Those points sat at the top
        of the explorer's Pareto front and set its axis. Any circuit that comes out
        better than Carnot between its own two temperatures is now refused, and the
        refusal is shown to the reader as a reason rather than a gap.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Standing<span>what this is and is not</span></div>
    <div class="col measure">
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          <b>This is a replication, not a verdict.</b> Every statement above is about
          <em>this</em> run against the numbers that paper reports, which is the only
          claim a replication is entitled to. Where a check failed, the failure is a
          statement about what can be reproduced from a published description — not
          about whether the original result is true.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>The whole operating envelope is explorable.</b> Every finding above sits at
          one point in a four-dimensional space of operating choices.
          <a href="/notes/cascade-explorer/">Move the four controls</a> and watch
          efficiency and annual cost trade against each other. Nothing there is
          interpolated: the two circuits are separable, so the same model recomputes
          every point in the browser, and the economic assumptions are controls too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The code is public and MIT licensed:
        <a href="https://github.com/taufik-adinugraha/openstudy">github.com/taufik-adinugraha/openstudy</a>,
        under <code>cases/cascade-cold/</code>. Two commands reproduce every number on this
        page. If a check here is wrong, that is
        <a href="/contribute/#attack">the most useful thing you could tell us</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .hero { padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .hero h1 { max-width: 24ch; }
  .sec .col.wide { max-width: none; }
  .tally {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(9rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1px; margin: 1.9rem 0 0; padding: 0;
    background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .tally > div { background: var(--ground); padding: 0.85rem 1rem; }
  .tally dt {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.66rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
  }
  .tally dd {
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.6rem;
    font-weight: 600; line-height: 1; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .tally dd.want { color: var(--flag); text-shadow: 0 0 22px var(--glow-flag); }
</style>
